{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310858,"nonce":"xBTzkvUnugWjG35YL4ZV2fS9LoI=","title":"Q5_0 GGUF→INT8-QTensor support (Qwen Q4_K_M 25.2 tok/s, 2.4x over Q6_K) + THE instance-extern receiver-position ABI fix (matmul_qt_t family ...","message":"Qwen2-0.5B hidden=896 is NOT divisible by 256 → k-quants (Q4_K/Q6_K) can't encode its attn/ffn tensors. The Q6_K GGUF ships them as **Q8_0 → loader dequants to F32** → most matmuls ran the F32 path (~10 tok/s). The \"Q4_K_M\" GGUF is really **Q5_0-dominant** (132 tensors: all attn q/k/output + ffn gate/up; token_embd Q8_0; ffn_down Q4_K/Q6_K). Inventory tool: scratchpad `gguf_inv.py \u003cfile\u003e` (dtype histogram per tensor).\n\nNo Q5_0 kernel needed: at load, each row is block-dequanted (22-byte/32-elem, f16 d + qh 5th-bits + nibbles — decode bit-exact vs ggml ref, unit-tested) then `quantise_int8_row` into the EXISTING QSCHEME_INT8 (per-row scale). Matmul: new `int8_xtq_threaded` short-circuit at top of `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded` (qmatmul_prep REJECTS INT8 — it's 256-block machinery): quantise x rows to i8 once, then `dot_i8_i8` (vdotq_s32 SDOT, in runtime-core int8.rs — runtime-core has the nightly `stdarch_neon_dotprod` feature; rayzor-tensors does NOT) per output row via parallel_rows. Haxe: `QTensor.fromBytesQ5_0Int8`; GGUFReader dtype-6 byte-size (22/32, shift-safe); GGUFLoader case 6 → setQuant; Q4Matmul INT8 guards route to matmulXTQThreaded (band loop is k-quant-only; matmulFused anyInt8 → per-weight split). Native fused-QKV gate-misses INT8 → three-call fallback ✓ by design.\n\n**Numbers (M1, rested, 300-tok decode):** Qwen Q4_K_M **25.2 tok/s ttft 0.19s** vs Qwen Q6_K 10.0-10.8 (**2.4×**); Llama Q4_K_M 85-86 unchanged; coherent (hash-map explainer + Paris/ChatML/finish=stop). REMEMBER heat-soak: back-to-back runs read −25% (Llama 64 heat-soaked vs 86 rested).\n\n`rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32{,_threaded,_chunk}` had Rust signatures **(x_tensor, qt_w, …)** — receiver SECOND. The plugin marshaling convention prepends SELF as native param 0 → the fn received x in the weight slot (garbage decode: scheme=x.numel low byte, rows=0xFFFFFFFF00000000=numa−1 — signature of reading a Tensor through QTensor offsets). **Broken for EVERY caller since the tensor-plugin migration, masked because default NUE_MATMUL=1 uses the Haxe band kernel and nothing else called these** (AMX-prefill early-return is opt-in; verified prod Llama NUE_MATMUL=0 crashed too). FIX: flipped the three signatures to self-first (qt_w, x, …) — uniform convention, no Haxe changes (manifest [Ptr,Ptr,…] = (self, x, …) unchanged). RULE: **an instance extern's native fn MUST take the receiver as param 0**; grep new plugin fns for this.\n\n`interface_call_result_types` is keyed by IrId REGISTER — only unique per function; it was never cleared → stale entries collided across functions and my [[project_conversation_api_and_iface_return_fix]] coercions consulted it in every Let/assign. FIX: clear it wherever `symbol_map.clear()` marks a fresh body (12 sites, hir_to_mir). Over-clearing degrades to W0014-hint behavior; under-clearing corrupts.\n\n1. **Extern method on FIELD-loaded receiver mis-converts non-Int returns**: `bias.numel()` (Float return) emitted `bitcast i64→f64` (bit reinterpret! prints 0) instead of numeric conversion — param-receiver path converts correctly (cast i64→i32→f64). Also `scheme():QScheme` enum-map → prints null via field receiver, \"0\"(raw) via param. MIR dump shows it plainly (RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR=1). Repro: /tmp/qwtest (Lin mirror of Linear w/ real Tensor/QTensor externs). Cost me hours as a LYING DIAGNOSTIC — prints said \"field corrupt\" when the field was fine.\n2. **Null\u003cHAXE-class\u003e field store+read SIGSEGVs** in the same shape (extern-class fields fine) — matches [[bugs_null_class_field_and_onearg_cast_xmodule]]; /tmp/qwtest with Payload class = instant repro.\n\n- Bundle traces are BUFFERED; native eprintln/Sys.println are not → \"crash before [meta]\" was really crash-in-first-forward with unflushed traces. Don't trust print ORDER across streams.\n- `Linear.forward` now THROWS a named diagnostic on null matmul (branch flags) — turned an opaque `add_into dest=0x0` abort into a one-line answer. Keep loud null-guards at FFI result boundaries.\n- Garbage-struct fingerprinting: rows=0xFFFFFFFF00000000 ⇒ you're reading a RayzorTensor (numa_node=-1) through RayzorQTensor offsets; scheme byte = victim's numel low byte identifies WHICH tensor got passed.\n- Repro harness /tmp/qwtest pattern (rayzor.toml classpaths → rayzor-tensors/haxe + native-libs dylib) iterates compiler/ABI bugs in ~10s vs minutes for the bundle; ALWAYS `find -name .rayzor -exec rm -rf` between runs (stale caches gave inverted results TWICE this session).\n- Bisect my-change-vs-pre-existing via `git stash push \u003cfile\u003e` + rebuild (~95s) — proved fromQuant corruption predated my compiler fixes.\n\nFollow-ups queued: inferred-scalar iface-return direct-use bug (annotate :Int works); per-token leak arc; Q8_0 2D-weights → INT8 QTensor routing (would also speed the Q6_K file's F32 path); wasm *_arr shims. [[feedback_always_report_qwen_tps]] [[feedback_benchmark_process_hygiene]]\n\nThe \"inferred scalar iface-return direct-use garbage\" bug ([[project_conversation_api_and_iface_return_fix]] OPEN item) is FIXED — full matrix green: S1 `var c=iface.count(); return c`, S4 trailing return, S5 reassign, S6 `(\"v=\"+c)==\"v=42\"`, S7 direct return, U1 `s.length`, plus all object shapes. THE LESSON: **symbol-identity tracking FAILED (SymbolId drift across lowering passes — Let-insert saw sym 370, return-check saw 287/853); register evidence is the durable currency.** Three mechanisms (hir_to_mir):\n1. **`boxed_value_regs`** — per-function BTreeSet\u003cIrId\u003e recorded by a `maybe_box_value` wrapper (inner renamed `maybe_box_value_inner`), cleared with symbol_map at the 12 body-boundary sites, **cast-transparent membership** (one-level Cast/BitCast provenance scan). `maybe_unbox_optional_for_target` consults it: a KNOWN box gets `haxe_unbox_int/float_ptr` even when its register is Ptr(Void) — previously the \"*void = raw enum payload\" heuristic (metaInt arc) bitcast the BOX POINTER (S1/S4 garbage). Both value families share Ptr(Void); only provenance disambiguates.\n2. **`lower_field_access` Dynamic arm**: receiver register typed String/`Ptr(String)` → direct `haxe_string_length` (+box result if field_ty erased) instead of the unbox_ref+reflect walk that read garbage off a RAW concat-produced string. (Concat itself was always correct; `.length`=0 was the reflect walk.)\n3. **`resolve_expr_ir_type`**: Variable whose default converts to erased Ptr(Void) → return symbol_map's bound-register type. Makes the existing string-compare gate see Ptr(String) → `haxe_string_compare` (S6 `==` was comparing POINTERS).\nAlso: scalar boxing at erased-hint Lets is KEPT (raw-scalar binding was tried and REVERTED — it broke Dynamic-protocol consumers: concat built length-0 strings; box + fix-the-consumers is the right shape).\n\n`Std.int(bias.numel())` via a FIELD receiver returned 4 instead of 404 (probe). ROOT: the field-receiver extern arm (hir_to_mir ~16409, `expected_return_type_qn` path) returned the call result at its NATIVE width (numel: native I64, Haxe-declared `Int`) with no narrowing. Downstream, `IrBuilder::build_call_direct`'s arg adaptation (builder.rs ~424) treats an `I64 -\u003e F64` pairing as **generic type erasure and BITCASTs it** (comment says \"bit-preserving\") — correct for Map\u003cK,Float\u003e raw slots, catastrophic for a genuine integer (i64 bits read as f64 ≈ 0). The working param-receiver path had narrowed i64→i32 first, so I32→F64 took the value-cast branch.\nFIX: new `reconcile_extern_return(value, native_ty, declared_ty)` helper — value-casts between NUMERIC scalars only (Any/ptr/void left alone: those are real erased slots). Wired at BOTH extern-return sites (the field-receiver arm + plugin_match, replacing an earlier ad-hoc match). Verified field==param==404, Float-typed return 6, matmul shapes unchanged.\n**RULE: an extern's native return width is not its Haxe type — reconcile at the callsite, or a downstream signature match will bit-reinterpret it.**\nTechnique: `#[track_caller]` + `Location::caller()` on `build_bitcast` (env-gated, F64 only) named the emitter in ONE run; same trick on `build_call_direct` with a name filter named the dispatch arm. This is the third time track_caller cracked a multi-arm dispatch mystery — reach for it FIRST.\n\nStill open (separate, non-blocking): `Std.string(enumFromExtern)` prints `null` on BOTH field and param receivers — an extern→enum *display/reflection* gap, NOT dispatch. Enum COMPARISONS are correct (`scheme()==QScheme.INT8` true / Q4 true), which is what Q4Matmul + the Q5_0 INT8 routing actually rely on — verified explicitly.\n\nValidation after all compiler edits: rayzor-tensors 53/53; qwtest int8+q4km matmul 1x4 ✓; bundle Llama \"Paris…\" finish=stop + Qwen coherent both files; TPS Qwen Q4_K_M 24.6-25.2 / Q6_K 10.6 / Llama 85.9 rested (71 mid-soak = thermal, not regression). fmt+clippy clean (2 pre-existing runtime/ lints).\n\n**The reported \"2.9GB and climbing 0.1GB/s\" was mostly NOT a leak.** Three separate things:\n1. **`ps` RSS lies on macOS** — it reported 2.9-3.1GB while `vmmap -summary`'s **Physical footprint** (the real pressure metric) said 1.2G. The delta is clean file-backed mmap pages (the GGUF is mmap'd via `File.getBytes` -\u003e `haxe_bytes_mmap_file`, and `tensorBytes` returns `subWithBase` VIEWS — weights are genuinely zero-copy for k-quants). **ALWAYS use `vmmap -summary | Physical footprint`, never ps RSS, on Mac.**\n2. **The 2.9GB was the Q6_K FILE**: Qwen Q6_K ships attn/ffn as Q8_0, which the loader DEQUANTS TO F32 → MALLOC_LARGE 1.4-1.8G, footprint **2.8G**. The Q4_K_M file on the new Q5_0→INT8 path is **1.6G** — my Q5_0 work roughly HALVED Qwen's memory as a side effect. (Remaining lever: route Q8_0 2D weights → INT8 too; would cut the Q6_K file the same way.)\n3. **A real, previously-unknown per-token growth WAS found and fixed** — but it was MINE: `int8_xtq_threaded` allocated a fresh `Vec\u003ci8\u003e`+`Vec\u003cf32\u003e` per call (~168 calls/token on 24 layers). Symptom: MALLOC_SMALL climbing monotonically 280→341MB (+61MB / ~1000 tok) and never plateauing. FIX: `INT8_X_SCRATCH` thread-local `(Vec\u003ci8\u003e, Vec\u003cf32\u003e)`, mirroring the existing `X_Q8K_SCRATCH` pattern (resize-if-smaller, reused across calls; `parallel_rows` joins before returning so worker raw-views can't outlive the borrow). AFTER: MALLOC_SMALL oscillates 242→213→234→167 (bounded sawtooth, comes back DOWN) and footprint is **pinned flat at 1.6G for a 40s/1148-token run**. TPS unchanged (Qwen 26.1, Llama 89.5 rested).\n\n**Tensors are NOT leaking**: `RZT_LEAK_STATS=1` shows `tensor_net_live` FLAT at 1.3-1.4MB across 180k allocations / 800 tokens. The old \"~390KB/tok residual\" framing in [[bugs_pool_data_flow_gap]] does not reproduce at HEAD on this workload.\n**Leak oracle now works on Mac** (was the reason \"NUC glibc shows, Mac hides\"): `proc_rss_mb()` was `/proc/self/status`-only → -1.0 on macOS. Added a `#[cfg(target_os=\"macos\")]` arm using `proc_pidinfo(PROC_PIDTASKINFO)` for `pti_resident_size`, plus `RZT_LEAK_INTERVAL` (default 20k) to control report frequency. Also confirmed: per-token peak RSS is FLAT across 20/200/600-token runs — no per-token leak on Llama.\n\nUser report: \"llama used to run at less than 900mb, now ceils at 1.3gb\". **Bisected empirically:\n`git stash push -u` ALL session changes + full rebuild + rebundle =\u003e STILL 1.3G.** So nothing this\nsession caused it. ROOT CAUSE: `runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs` mmap preload path **mlock()s the whole\nmodel mapping** (was `RAYZOR_NO_MLOCK_MMAP` opt-OUT, i.e. wired by default). Wired pages are\npermanently resident + non-reclaimable and are charged in full to Physical footprint: 770MB for\nLlama-1B Q4_K_M. FIX: flipped to **opt-IN `RAYZOR_MLOCK_MMAP=1`** (legacy `RAYZOR_NO_MLOCK_MMAP`\nstill forces off), preserving the original rationale in the comment.\n**Llama 1.3G -\u003e 401MB; Qwen Q4_K_M 1.5G -\u003e 979MB; TPS UNCHANGED** (Llama 82.7-85.9, Qwen 25.8).\nThe mlock was added for a 92%-swap-full 16GB box (eviction mid-decode -\u003e 60-236ms p95 stalls);\non an unpressured host preloaded pages stay resident anyway, so wiring bought nothing measurable.\n\n1. **Never sample vmmap during a timed run.** My footprint loop called `vmmap -summary` every 1s;\n   on a 1.3GB process that scans the whole address space and CRUSHED throughput (Llama read 39.6\n   and Qwen 16.5 tok/s). Re-measured with sampling removed: 87.0 / 23.0 — no regression at all.\n   MEASURE MEMORY AND SPEED IN SEPARATE RUNS. Also check `ps -A -o %cpu,comm | sort -rn | head`\n   first — WindowServer was at 52% during the bad runs.\n2. **Static analysis lost to a one-line empirical check.** A 5-agent audit concluded with HIGH\n   confidence that my ABI fix had re-enabled `cached_f16_weight` (quant.rs:1267 — a leak-by-design\n   f16 weight cache, `Box::leak`, \"never freed\", ~1.65GB for the 1B model), because that cache sits\n   behind `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded`, which WAS dead before the fix. Reality:\n   `RZT_AMX_DEBUG=1` printed **0** `[amx-dequant] MISS` lines and 0 `[amx-gate]` lines, and\n   `RZT_AMX_PREFILL=0` changed footprint by nothing. WHY: prefill runs on the **CoreML\n   PrefillGraph** (`[prefill] engine=graph on`, ttft 0.10s) which bypasses the CPU matmul path\n   entirely, so the AMX branch never executes. Plausible dead-code reasoning != execution.\n\n- If AMX prefill ever DOES run (graph off / non-Mac-graph host) with `NUE_PREFILL_LAST_LOGITS=0`,\n  lm_head [128256,2048] would add a single **525MB** leaked f16 entry (+1.05GB transient f32\n  staging). Today forwardLastLogits slices to batch=1 so it can't trigger.\n- `cached_f16_weight_f32` (quant.rs:1393) is keyed by RAW DATA POINTER — a freed+reallocated\n  weight buffer at the same address would hit a stale entry. Key by something stable if it's ever\n  put on a live path.\n\n`runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs`: `mem_facts()` + pure `decide_mlock(len, facts, is_macos)`, 7 unit tests\n(`mlock_policy_tests`) covering idle host / swap-pressured host / macOS / container / unlimited\nsentinel / oversized model / unknown facts. Policy in one line: **wire only on a non-macOS,\nnon-cgroup-limited host that is actually distressed (MemAvailable \u003c 20% or swap \u003e 50% used), and\nnever wire more than half the effective budget.** Env still overrides both ways\n(`RAYZOR_MLOCK_MMAP=1` / `RAYZOR_NO_MLOCK_MMAP=1`). Live: Llama auto=393MB, forced=1.2G.\nLlama 89.3 tok/s, Qwen 27.0 tok/s after the change.\n- **macOS never auto-wires** — no cheap signal reports PRESENT pressure there:\n  `vm.swapusage.xsu_used` is monotonic compressor history since boot (this box reads 85-93% swap\n  used while serving 87 tok/s), `vm.page_free_count` is ~59MB by design with GBs compressed, and\n  `kern.memorystatus_vm_pressure_level` reads WARN(2) when idle. Only CRITICAL(4) is meaningful,\n  and by then you want to shrink, not pin.\n- **cgroup-limited hosts never auto-wire**: under a hard ceiling, unreclaimable pages turn the\n  failure mode from \"slow\" into \"OOM-killed\". `/proc/meminfo` reports HOST memory inside a\n  container, so the cgroup v2/v1 limit takes precedence when lower (a unit test asserts this).\n- Use `libc::xsw_usage` (verified present, 32 bytes, `xsu_encrypted` is `boolean_t`=i32) — my\n  hand-rolled struct had it as u8 and would have mis-sized the sysctl buffer.\n**MEASUREMENT CORRECTION for the record: this dev Mac is NOT unpressured** (16GB, swap 85-93%\nused) — it IS the regime mlock was designed for, and wiring still bought nothing: interleaved A/B\nmedians 87.2 unwired vs 86.7 wired. Single runs on this box swing 69-88 tok/s, so ALWAYS\ninterleave A/B and take medians here; one-shot comparisons are worthless.\n\n- **`libc::xsw_usage` exists** (libc 0.2.186 apple/mod.rs:636), byte-identical to the SDK's\n  struct: 32 bytes, `xsu_used` already in BYTES, `xsu_encrypted` is `boolean_t`=**i32** (NOT a\n  bool byte). My hand-rolled version had u8 there and would have mis-sized the sysctl buffer.\n- **Do NOT read `vm.page_free_count` with an 8-byte helper** — sysctl widths are inconsistent\n  per-key: page_free_count and page_speculative_count are u32 (4B) while `vm.page_purgeable_count`\n  is u64 (8B). I now report `available: 0` (= unknown) on macOS instead, because free memory is\n  not a meaningful availability figure there at all (measured 0.33-1.24% free on a HEALTHY 16GB\n  box with ~6GB in the compressor — any \"available \u003c X\" rule fires permanently).\n- **RLIMIT_MEMLOCK is the mlock gate on LINUX ONLY.** macOS reports `ulimit -l unlimited` and\n  gates on the Mach wire limit (`vm.global_user_wire_limit`) instead. So: (a) Linux now\n  pre-checks getrlimit(RLIMIT_MEMLOCK) and declines rather than attempting a wire that can only\n  fail+warn; (b) the failure message names the right knob per platform (it previously told macOS\n  users to raise RLIMIT_MEMLOCK, which does nothing there).\n- Better macOS pressure signals IF one is ever needed (not needed today, since macOS never\n  auto-wires): `vm.compressor_bytes_used` (u64 BYTES, private KPI but long-present — the earliest\n  honest indicator, and unlike swap it is not sticky for hours), or `host_statistics64(HOST_VM_INFO64)`\n  into `libc::vm_statistics64` (mach_host_self/host_statistics64/HOST_VM_INFO64_COUNT are all in\n  libc, un-cfg'd). `kern.memorystatus_vm_pressure_level` reads WARN(2) when idle — only CRITICAL(4)\n  is actionable, and by then you want to shrink, not pin. macOS swap files are allocated LAZILY, so\n  `xsu_used \u003e 0` IS meaningful there (unlike Linux) but STICKY — it answers \"was this host recently\n  short\", not \"is it short now\".\nFinal: Llama 89.0 tok/s / auto 393MB, Qwen 26.4 tok/s. fmt+clippy clean (2 pre-existing runtime lints).\n\nExtracted the Linux-only code (MemFacts / linux_mem_facts_from / mem_facts / decide_mlock +\nthe RLIMIT_MEMLOCK pre-check) VERBATIM into a standalone crate, rsync'd it, and built+ran it\nwith the NUC's own rustc 1.93.1 (`cargo build --offline`, libc from the local registry cache):\n  - **compiles clean on real x86_64 Linux** (the macOS box cfg's it out and the cross-build is\n    blocked by an unrelated C dep, so this was the open risk — now closed);\n  - live `mem_facts()`: total 6981MB, available 5658MB, swap 421/4095MB, cgroup_limited=false;\n  - `decide_mlock(770MB)` = **false** (healthy host: 81% available, swap 10%) — correct;\n  - all **7 policy unit tests pass on Linux**.\n**THE NUC PROVES THE FIX MATTERS**: `ulimit -l` there is **8192 KB (8 MB)**, so mlock of a 770MB\nmodel can NEVER succeed on that box. Ran the NUC's existing OLD binary (it only knows the\nopt-OUT `RAYZOR_NO_MLOCK_MMAP`, i.e. mlock default-ON) and observed, every run:\n  `warning: mlock of 770 MB model mapping failed (errno 12 = ENOMEM); pages stay evictable ...`\nSo the competition target was paying a failed syscall + a scary warning on every single startup\nAND never getting the eviction protection mlock existed for. The new pre-check declines before\nattempting, so: no failed syscall, no warning, same (correct) unwired outcome.\nNUC access recipe: `ssh nuc` (alias in ~/.ssh/config -\u003e raikage@192.168.1.181), repo ~/rayzor,\nmodel ~/llama-q4.gguf (807MB), bundle + libnue_plugins.so/librayzor_tensors.so already built.\nStandalone-extract + `cargo --offline` is the CHEAP way to validate a Linux-only code path from\nthe Mac without syncing/rebuilding the whole tree.\n\nValidated on a REAL model — downloaded `Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct-GGUF\nqwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-q5_k_m.gguf` (498MB) to /Users/amaterasu/models/qwen/.\n**Numbers: Qwen Q5_K_M 26.9 tok/s (FASTEST of the three Qwen quants: Q4_K_M 24.5, Q6_K ~10.6),\ncoherent over 300 tokens, footprint 1.1G. Llama 85.2 unchanged. 58/58 tests.**\n\n- **32-element legacy blocks (Q5_0, Q5_1)** -\u003e INT8 per-row. They cannot ride the 256-wide\n  k-quant machinery at all. Q5_1 = Q5_0 plus an explicit min: `y = d*q + m`, NOT `d*(q-16)`.\n- **256-element k-quant superblocks (Q5_K)** -\u003e re-encode to **Q4_K_M**, NOT INT8. Q5_K is\n  5.5 bits/weight; INT8 would EXPAND it to 8, while Q4_K_M is 4.5 -\u003e the weights SHRINK, keep\n  their per-32-element scale AND min (INT8 flattens a whole row onto one symmetric scale), and\n  ride the fastest kernel in the tree (Q4_K SDOT, AMX-eligible). Costs 1 bit of mantissa at load.\n  Reuses the existing `quantize_block_q4_k_m` + the `requant_q6k_to_q4km` rayon pattern.\n\n176 bytes / 256 weights: `d`(f16) `dmin`(f16) `scales[12]` `qh[32]` `qs[128]`.\nThe 12-byte scales use the SAME 6-bit packing as Q4_K -\u003e **reuse `q4_k_get_scale_min` verbatim**.\nTHE HIGH-RISK DETAIL (unit-tested explicitly): `qh` is **not advanced** between groups — the MASK\nshifts. Walk 256 weights in 4 groups of 64 (two 32-elem sub-blocks sharing 32 nibble bytes);\ngroup g consumes bit 2g (low half) and bit 2g+1 (high half) of `qh[l]`; `ql += 32` per group,\n`u1 \u003c\u003c= 2, u2 \u003c\u003c= 2`. Value form is Q4_K's: `y = d*sc*(q + 16*bit) - dmin*mn` (min SUBTRACTED).\nQ5_1: 24 bytes/32 — d(f16), m(f16), qh[4], qs[16]; low elem j uses qh bit j, HIGH elem j (output\nj+16) uses qh bit **j+16** (`(qh\u003e\u003e(j+12))\u00260x10`). My first test asserted bit 19 for high elem 7\nand the test correctly failed — the DECODER was right, the test was wrong. Watch that indexing.\n\n1. **`rayzor_tensor_gather_rows_q6_k` was Q6_K-ONLY but `Embedding.lookup` routes EVERY quantised\n   embedding through it** (`if (qweight != null) qweight.gatherRowsQ6K(...)`, no scheme check).\n   This file's `token_embd` is Q5_1 -\u003e INT8 -\u003e gather returned 0 -\u003e null Tensor -\u003e **SIGSEGV on the\n   first forward**. Broadened it to INT8 + Q4_K_M + Q6_K (name is now historical). Any model whose\n   token_embd is Q4_K would have crashed the same way.\n2. **`auto_kernel_threads()` was called EAGERLY** in `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded` (2\n   sites) even when `threads \u003e 0`. It resolves `perf_core_count` from the host binary via\n   RTLD_GLOBAL, which is ABSENT under `cargo test` -\u003e null fn pointer -\u003e SIGSEGV. That is why the\n   Q4_K path had no end-to-end test. Made lazy; the Q5_K end-to-end matmul test now runs.\n\nLike \"Q4_K_M\", the name is a MIX. This one: Q5_1 x133 (incl. token_embd), Q5_K x12 (only\n`ffn_down`, the one dim divisible by 256), Q8_0 x13 (output.weight), Q6_K x12, F32 x121. So Q5_K\nsupport alone would NOT have loaded it — Q5_1 was the majority. ALWAYS run scratchpad\n`gguf_inv.py \u003cfile\u003e` first; never trust the filename.\nFootprint 1.1G for a 498MB file is dominated by `output.weight` Q8_0 -\u003e F32 (151936x896x4 = 545MB)\n— which is exactly the queued Q8_0-2D -\u003e INT8 lever, now worth much more than it looked.\n\nAn independent verifier re-derived the layouts against ggml and the in-tree code: **no error that\nwould produce silent garbage**; block size, the reused `q4_k_get_scale_min`, the qh mapping, the\nsubtracted-dmin form and the per-row `cols % 256` guard all confirmed. It found real gaps in MY\nTESTS instead, all now closed:\n1. the one-hot qh probe pinned `probe_l = 5` (8 of 256 bits) -\u003e now sweeps all 32 byte positions\n   x 8 bits = **full bijectivity proof** of the qh plane onto the 256 weights;\n2. **every sc6/mn6 value in the Q5_K tests was \u003c 16**, so the `j \u003e= 4` branch of\n   `q4_k_get_scale_min` (which ORs in two spill bits from the high bits of bytes 0..8) was never\n   exercised -\u003e now uses sc6=[1,2,3,4,60,61,62,63] / mn6=[63,62,61,60,4,3,2,1].\n   **MUTATION-VERIFIED**: deleting the spill term makes the test fail; restoring it passes. A test\n   that passes is not the same as a test with teeth — mutate to prove coverage.\n3. Q5_1 probe now covers the shift extremes (bits 0/15/16/31), where an off-by-4 in\n   `(qh \u003e\u003e (j+12)) \u0026 0x10` hides.\nWhy this matters: a wrong qh mapping is the WORST failure mode here — it is a permutation of a bit\nworth exactly +16 on a 0..31 quant, so the value distribution, the dmin term and the logit\nmagnitudes all stay plausible and generation stays FLUENT. Every other error is louder (bad block\nsize mis-slices immediately; a -16/-32 bias carryover is a visible DC shift).\nFinal numbers (rested): Qwen Q5_K_M 24.6-26.9, Q4_K_M 26.1, Llama 82.8-85.2 tok/s; 58/58 tests.\n\nQ8_0 was the last dtype still dequantised to F32 at load. It is ALREADY int8 with a per-32 f16\nscale, so wrapping it as a QTensor view over the mapped file (owns_data=false, like Q4_K_M/Q6_K)\nis bit-exact and costs ZERO extra bytes — vs a ~3.8x expansion AND the F32 matmul path.\n**THIS ANSWERS \"why is Qwen 3x slower than a model twice its size\":** Qwen's 152k-vocab\nlm_head/token_embd ships as Q8_0, so the LARGEST matmul per token (896 x 151936) ran in F32 every\nsingle step. Fixing it moved every Qwen quant at once:\n  Qwen Q6_K   ~10.0 -\u003e **85.2 tok/s**, 2.8G -\u003e **269MB**  (that file is 145/145 Q8_0)\n  Qwen Q5_K_M  24.6 -\u003e **88.6**,       1.1G -\u003e 785MB\n  Qwen Q4_K_M  24.5 -\u003e **95.3**,       1.5G -\u003e 627MB   \u003c- now FASTER than Llama-1B\n  Llama Q4_K_M 86.0 -\u003e 86.0 unchanged (has no Q8_0 tensors)\nDesign: only 2-D weights with `cols % 32 == 0` are wrapped (a QTensor's [out,in] shape is\nmeaningless for a 1-D norm/bias; the view needs 32-alignment). `NUE_Q8_0_QUANT=0` reverts.\n\nquant.rs: data_bytes(~154), alloc_qtensor sizing(~321), dequant(~1335), gather_rows(~1219),\nmatmul dispatch, qmatmul_prep(~2253), qmatmul_chunk_impl outer + 2 inner matches, deep_clone;\nplus lib.rs manifest+entry, QTensor.hx extern, **QScheme.hx (ordinals load-bearing — APPEND\nQ8_0=3, never reorder)**, and Q4Matmul's guards. Miss the Haxe enum and every\n`scheme()==QScheme.X` silently returns false and falls through to the Q4_K_M branch, which reads\n34-byte blocks as 144-byte super-blocks — for MiniLM k=384 that is `bpr = 384\u003e\u003e8 = 1`, visiting\n256 of 384 columns. No crash, no warning. Q4Matmul now has `isRowwise()` (INT8 or Q8_0) as the\nsingle \"must not enter the 256-wide band loop\" predicate.\nAlso defused the two `unreachable!()` arms in qmatmul_chunk_impl -\u003e `return`: they run under an\nextern \"C\" frame where a panic ABORTS rather than unwinds.\n\nThe audit predicted it and it was right: `BertArch.takeWeight` only read the F32 map and\n`makeLinear` never called `Linear.fromQuant`, so the instant Q8_0 became a QTensor every MiniLM\nmatmul weight went invisible — and `StringMap.get` returns a raw 0 for a missing key\n([[bugs_stringmap_null_get]]) so the null guard could not even be trusted. I added the quant path,\nand it SIGSEGVs at load (null fat-pointer vtable dispatch, `ldp x9,x0,[x8]` with x8=0) even with\nthe embedding routed through dequant — so the encoder needs real debugging, not a quick fix.\nRESOLUTION: BertArch dequants QTensors back to F32, reproducing its prior representation\nbit-for-bit (no regression, no gain), and `takeWeight` now THROWS a named error if a quantised\nweight reaches an F32-only call site instead of yielding a 0-handle. LLMs keep the full win.\nVerified: bert-embed on the all-Q8_0 MiniLM exits 0, dim=384.\n\n`dequant_preserves_block_order_in_linear_memory` failed once under parallel `cargo test`\n(\"alt-shape block0[0] = 0 (expected 1)\") right after the 3 new Q8_0 tests landed, then passed\nalone, single-threaded, and 6/6 parallel reruns. Suspect shared global state across concurrently\nrunning tests. If it resurfaces, chase test isolation rather than the Q4_K dequant.\n\nMINIMAL REPRO (this is the whole finding): take a KNOWN-GOOD `BertArch.build` and add ONE line —\n`var probe = Tensor.zeros([1, 1], rayzor.ds.DType.F32);` — change nothing else. Result: **exit 133\n(SIGTRAP, trap stub)**. **`nue.arch.BertArch` cannot call `Tensor.zeros` AT ALL.** Nothing to do\nwith quantisation, QTensors, Q8_0, the bias, or the static-factory call per se.\n`Linear.fromQuant` crashes from BertArch only because its sentinel line calls `Tensor.zeros`.\nBelongs to [[bugs_trap_stub_cascade]] / [[bugs_xmodule_resolution_disease_cluster]] (extern static\nnewly referenced from a module that never referenced it before).\n\nBisect that got there (all on the all-Q8_0 MiniLM, `nue/examples/bert-embed`):\n  `new Linear(qw.dequant(), bias, \"weight\")` + `lq.qweight = qw`   -\u003e **exit 0, dim=384** (works!)\n  `new Linear(Tensor.zeros([1,1],DType.F32), bias, ...)` + qweight -\u003e exit 133\n  `Linear.fromQuant(qw, bias, \"weight\")`                           -\u003e exit 139\n  same with null bias / `Tensor.uninit` / 1-D `zeros([1])`         -\u003e still crashes\nSo the quant path itself is FINE for BERT — only the sentinel allocation traps. A future fix can\nenable full BERT quant support by not allocating a sentinel inside `fromQuant` (e.g. take the\nsentinel from the caller) even before the compiler bug is fixed.\nFALSIFIED ALONG THE WAY (do not re-chase): the dangling-mmap theory (`NUE_FREE_GGUF_BYTES=0`\nchanged nothing), the bare-`F32`-enum theory (qualifying to `DType.F32` changed nothing), and\nBERT deriving dims from `Linear.weight.shape()` (it does not — dims are explicit from metadata).\nDEBUG NOTE: Haxe `Sys.println` traces are BUFFERED and LOST on SIGSEGV — 0 trace lines proved\nnothing. Rust-side `eprintln` is unbuffered and showed all 37 `from_bytes_q8_0` calls succeeding,\nwhich is what localised the crash to AFTER load.\n\n`haxe_qscheme_enum_matches_rust_tags` parses QScheme.hx and asserts variant name/count/ordinal\nagainst the QSCHEME_* constants. Mutation-verified both ways (delete a variant -\u003e \"has 3 variants\nbut Rust defines 4\"; swap two -\u003e \"order is load-bearing\"). This closes the silent-false hazard:\npreviously, adding a Rust scheme without the Haxe variant compiled cleanly and made every\n`scheme() == QScheme.X` false, falling through to the Q4_K_M branch.\nCONFIRMED SAFE (probed directly): with the enum in sync, `switch` and `==` are CORRECT for every\nscheme incl. Q8_0. Only `Std.string(externEnum)` still prints `null` (cosmetic, still open).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_q5_0_int8_and_xtq_abi_fix` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}